#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059476 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57187716-c00b-5428-a83a-18836d6b3d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Transpose of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "e3d1f202-a3d6-561f-bd4b-3e886142e5a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4386" :question/problem "Find $C^T,$ if \n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cf627a0-6921-58ee-91c0-2f225d425711" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Transpose of a Row Vector or Column Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c8ca81da-04d3-532f-94f4-5f43703c7216" :question/math-academy-id "e-4387" :question/problem "If\n    ${A} = \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        5 & -1 & 0 \\\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack,$\nthen what is ${A^T} ?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns. \n\nHere, there is just a single row, so it becomes a single column:\n\n$$\n{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\matrix {\n    5 \\ \n    -1 \\ \n    0 \\  \n    \t\t}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2418a8-b245-5600-8e7e-c54506e3f321" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of the Transpose of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "02372092-ed41-5533-8238-0361856b731d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4388" :question/problem "Consider the matrix\n    ${B} = \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        1 & 2 & 4 & 2\\\n        4 & 4 & 1 & 1\\\n        3 & -3 & 0 & -1\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack.$\nWhat are the dimensions of ${B}^T?$" :question/worked-solution "When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $N \\times M$ to $M \\times N.$\n\nSo, since $B$ is a $3 \\times 